// Fs.rho — File I/O FIP §The Fs agent.
//
// Top-level entry point.  Provisioned at genesis with a static bundle
// mapping logical names to
//   (canonRoot, rel, mode, kind, consensusMode)
// tuples (5-ary as of slice 26); the powerbox delegates the resulting
// Fs cap to deploys.  Deploys open files/dirs by their logical name,
// never by absolute host path.
//
// ## Delivered methods
//
//   constructor(stdinFd, stdoutFd, stderrFd, bMap)
//                                              — stash stdio fds + bundle
//   openFile(name, options)  → [true, fileCap] — look up + mode-cap + open (fresh mint)
//   openDir(name, options)   → [true, dirCap]  — look up + mode-cap + open (fresh mint)
//   stdin()   → [true, stdinCap]               — mint fresh Stdin(fd)
//   stdout()  → [true, stdoutCap]              — mint fresh Stdout(stdoutFd)
//   stderr()  → [true, stderrCap]              — mint fresh Stdout(stderrFd)
//   default(...@args)                           — mandatory catch-all
//
// Constructor arity/order is a coordination contract with the genesis
// wiring slice (19); do not change without updating StandardDeploys.
//
// ## Publication (slice 19)
//
// A single Fs instance is composed with the other library agents into
// FsGenesis (`casper/src/rust/genesis/contracts/fs_genesis.rs`), which
// runs at genesis and publishes the resulting `bundle+{*fs}` at the
// registry URI derived from `FS_GENERATOR_PK`.  Deploys reach the Fs
// via `getFS(rho:id:<hash>)`; the canonical spec URI
// `rho:io:fs:1.0.0` is NOT yet wired (tracked as PB-B-3 in
// FIPS/fileio/.../powerbox-requirements.md).  MVP simplification #1
// in the fs_genesis docstring notes the shared-Fs model — all deploys
// look up the same handle.  Per-principal delegation waits on the
// powerbox slice (PB-M-1).
//
// ## Stdio (slice 18 — spec §842-844, §867, §1085, §1114)
//
// Each `stdin()` / `stdout()` / `stderr()` call mints a FRESH Stdin
// (or Stdout) capability wrapping the fd the powerbox handed us at
// construction.  Two calls on the same Fs return DISTINCT caps —
// this matches spec §1085 ("a distinct Stdin cap obtained from
// Fs.stdin() later can still read") and §1114 ("A fresh Fs!stdout()
// call after close returns a new Stdout cap that CAN still write").
// Caching would break those clauses: a well-meaning `close()` would
// permanently disable stdio for the rest of the Fs's life.
//
// Cross-Fs isolation (spec §867) holds trivially: Alice's Fs holds
// its own `*private` cells with its own fd values; Bob's Fs holds
// its own.  Any minted Stdin / Stdout has a fresh `*private` scope
// on each `!?`, so the per-instance state cells inside Stdin.rho /
// Stdout.rho are disjoint even when two agents wrap the same fd.
// Closing Alice's cap doesn't affect Bob's.  NB: under the shared-Fs
// MVP (see §Publication above), all deploys hold the SAME Fs, so the
// "fresh cap after close" guarantee is per-cap-instance only — the
// underlying fd is shared across every deploy on the validator.
// Isolation returns to per-deploy scope when PB-M-1 lands.
//
// Ambient-authority note: Stdin and Stdout are module-level agents.
// The genesis wiring slice (19) MUST NOT export `Stdin` / `Stdout`
// to deploy scope — only `Fs` and its bundle+ interface.  Otherwise
// a deploy could bypass Fs.stdin() with a raw `Stdin!?(0)`.  See
// slice-19 acceptance criteria.
//
// Trust boundary: the constructor accepts fds as unchecked Pars.
// Genesis is responsible for supplying Int fds and a Map bMap; Fs
// treats them as opaque data and forwards to Stdin/Stdout mints
// (which likewise trust their fd) and to bMap lookups (which
// tolerate empty/missing entries via `.contains`).
//
// ## Fresh-mint semantics (slice 27)
//
// Every `openFile(name, options)` / `openDir(name, options)` returns
// a FRESH agent handle with an independent cursor and independent
// lifecycle — regardless of whether the same `(name, options)` pair
// was opened earlier on this Fs.  Slice 17 memoized handles per
// `(canonicalPath, resolvedMode)`; slice 27 (2026-08-04) reverted that
// on POSIX-semantics grounds: two `openFile(same-name, same-mode)`
// calls MUST yield distinct `File` agents with independent read/
// write positions, otherwise Alice's `read` would silently advance
// Bob's cursor.  Shared byte-level coordination is Phase 8's job
// (range locks), not Fs-level cap memoization.
//
// The `openFile` / `openDir` methods therefore invoke
// `openFileImpl` / `openDirImpl` (defined by Dir.rho and shared with
// nested Dir.openFile/openDir call sites) directly — no cache lookup,
// no cache-set on the success path.  This mirrors the slice-18 stdio
// design: each stdio method mints fresh, so its close doesn't
// invalidate subsequent calls.
//
// ## Bundle shape
//
// Each bundle entry is a 5-tuple `(canonRoot, rel, provisionedMode,
// kind, consensusMode)`:
//   canonRoot         — absolute directory (validated at load time to
//                       be absolute and symlink-free per spec §1269)
//   rel               — path within canonRoot; joinRel(subPath="", rel)
//                       used by openFileImpl / openDirImpl produces the
//                       full relative segment
//   provisionedMode   — max attenuation ("r" / "rw" for dirs; any file
//                       mode from spec §File modes for files)
//   kind              — "file" or "dir"
//   consensusMode     — "oracular" or "consensus" (slice 26).  Derived
//                       from the operator's config bucket
//                       (`oracle-static-*` vs `consensus-static-*`)
//                       and threaded through into the File/Dir
//                       constructor's state cell.  File/Dir chown/
//                       stat/entries pass it to the native handler,
//                       which short-circuits `chown` on consensus and
//                       omits host-transient fields from stat/entries
//                       records.
//
// The genesis wiring slice (19) converts the config-block shape
// `{"path": "...", "mode": "...", "consensusMode": "..."}` into these
// tuples before minting Fs (see casper/src/rust/genesis/contracts/
// fs_genesis.rs::format_bundle_for_rholang).
//
// ## Options handling
//
// `options` is a Map (spec §848).  Rholang has no lightweight `x /\
// Map` type predicate, so this MVP uses structural patterns:
//   `{}`                          → no options, default mode "r"
//   `{"mode": modeVal ..._}`      → mode explicitly set
//   `{_ : _ ..._}`                → non-empty Map without "mode" → default
//   any other shape               → FSERR_BAD_ARG "options must be a Map"
// Additional options like `create`, `exclusive`, `path` (spec §855-861)
// are accepted (captured in the wildcard remainder) but ignored in this
// slice.
//
// ## Mode-cap logic (spec §847)
//
// A downgrade succeeds; an upgrade → FSERR_UNSUPPORTED.  Mirrors
// Dir.openFile / Dir.openDir:
//   - Request `"r"` is always allowed.
//   - Any other requested mode requires provisioned `"rw"` (or the
//     analogous write-capable dir mode).
//
// ## Known limitation — narrow-provisioned mode-cap (m-16-5)
//
// The "r" always-allowed rule means a `"w"`-only provisioned entry
// would still grant `"r"` reads — technically wider than spec §847's
// strict "capped at provisioned" reading.  Matches the pre-existing
// Dir.openFile behavior.
//
// ## m-16-6 fix (2026-08-06, M-6 rev-2)
//
// Pre-fix, the File openFile cap check was `provisioned == "rw"`.
// But `CONFIG_FILE_MODES = {"r", "r+", "w", "w+", "a", "a+"}` —
// no `"rw"` — so File entries could NEVER be provisioned as
// `"rw"`, and every non-`"r"` file open through `Fs.openFile`
// unconditionally returned `FSERR_UNSUPPORTED` in production.
// The pre-existing tests only passed because they hand-craft a
// fake bundle with a File entry provisioned as `"rw"` — a shape
// no HOCON- or CLI-parsed bundle can produce.  A real deployment
// with `oracle-static-files { "cfg" = { path = ..., mode = "r+" }}`
// followed by `Fs.openFile("cfg", {"mode": "w"})` returned
// `FSERR_UNSUPPORTED` with `"requested mode exceeds provisioned
// attenuation"` — even though the operator granted write access.
//
// Post-fix cap check: `provisioned == "r"` rejects non-`"r"`
// requests; any other provisioned mode (`r+`, `w`, `w+`, `a`,
// `a+`, and the non-spec but still-accepted `rw`) allows the
// request through.  Matches the File.rho write-capable-mode
// pattern already used at every internal method gate
// (writeChars, writeLines, chmod, chown, truncate, etc.).
//
// The requested-mode arm retains `"rw"` as an accepted request
// even though it is not in the spec's File modes table — the
// native mode parser (mode.rs::parse_open_mode) accepts `"rw"`
// as an alias for POSIX `"r+"` (ReadWrite + require-exist).
// The spec/impl drift on `"rw"` vs `"r+"` is tracked as a
// separate cleanup; retaining `"rw"` as a valid request
// preserves existing caller code and matches native semantics.
//
// ## Map pattern-match ordering assumption
//
// `{"mode": modeVal ..._}` extracts the `"mode"` key from a Map
// regardless of insertion order.  Rholang's current tuplespace Map
// implementation supports keyed extraction independent of order; if
// that ever changes, this pattern would silently stop matching Maps
// where `"mode"` isn't the first key.  Add an ordering-independent
// probe (`.contains` + `.get`) here if the guarantee is ever weakened.
//
// ## Not-in-bundle
//
// `openFile("nope", ...)` and `openDir("nope", ...)` both fail with
// `[false, "FSERR_UNSUPPORTED", "logical name not in static bundle"]`
// per spec §846.

new Fs, fsBundleP,
    fsStdinFdP, fsStdoutFdP, fsStderrFdP in {

  agent Fs {
    // -- constructor(stdinFd, stdoutFd, stderrFd, bMap) -----------
    //
    // Powerbox provides the three stdio fds and the static bundle at
    // mint time.  Fds are stashed unchanged; each stdio method mints
    // a fresh Stdin / Stdout on demand (spec §1085 / §1114 require
    // fresh-cap-after-close).
    constructor(@stdinFd, @stdoutFd, @stderrFd, @bMap) {
      @[*private, *fsBundleP]!(bMap) |
      @[*private, *fsStdinFdP]!(stdinFd) |
      @[*private, *fsStdoutFdP]!(stdoutFd) |
      @[*private, *fsStderrFdP]!(stderrFd)
    } |

    // -- stdin() / stdout() / stderr() ----------------------------
    //
    // Fresh-mint per call.  Two calls on the same Fs return DISTINCT
    // caps; closing one does not affect any subsequent call.  Fd
    // cell is read non-linearly so unbounded parallel calls are safe.
    //
    // ## Consensus discipline — stdio is INTRINSICALLY ORACULAR
    //
    // The Stdin / Stdout constructors take a fd only, no `cmode`
    // argument (see Stdin.rho / Stdout.rho docstrings for full
    // rationale).  Consensus-mode deploys are structurally still
    // able to call `Fs.stdin()` / `Fs.stdout()` / `Fs.stderr()` —
    // there is no runtime gate here — but any bytes read from
    // stdin or side-effects observed via stdout MUST NOT flow into
    // consensus-observable tuplespace state.  A deploy that routes
    // stdin bytes into consensus state will diverge across nodes
    // and fail consensus with a state-root mismatch.  This is a
    // deploy-author invariant, consistent with the treatment of
    // oracular file reads.
    method stdin() {
      for (@fd <<- @[*private, *fsStdinFdP]) {
        for (@cap <- Stdin!?(fd)) {
          return!([true, cap])
        }
      }
    } |

    method stdout() {
      for (@fd <<- @[*private, *fsStdoutFdP]) {
        for (@cap <- Stdout!?(fd)) {
          return!([true, cap])
        }
      }
    } |

    method stderr() {
      for (@fd <<- @[*private, *fsStderrFdP]) {
        for (@cap <- Stdout!?(fd)) {
          return!([true, cap])
        }
      }
    } |

    // -- openFile(name, options) -----------------------------------
    //
    // Slice 27: fresh-mint per call — every invocation invokes
    // `openFileImpl` directly (no cache lookup).  Two calls with the
    // same `(name, options)` yield DISTINCT File agents with
    // independent cursors; closing one has no effect on the other.
    method openFile(@name, @options) {
      match name {
        n /\ String => {
          new modeCh in {
            match options {
              {} => modeCh!("r")
              {"mode": modeVal ..._} => modeCh!(modeVal)
              {_ : _ ..._} => modeCh!("r")
              _ => modeCh!(Nil)
            } |
            for (@requestedMode <- modeCh) {
              match requestedMode {
                Nil => return!(
                  [false, "FSERR_BAD_ARG", "options must be a Map"])
                m /\ String => {
                  for (@bMap <<- @[*private, *fsBundleP]) {
                    match bMap.contains(n) {
                      false => return!(
                        [false, "FSERR_UNSUPPORTED",
                         "logical name not in static bundle"])
                      true => {
                        match bMap.get(n) {
                          (canonRoot, rel, provisioned, "file", cmode) => {
                            match m {
                              "r" => openFileImpl!(
                                canonRoot, "", rel, "r", cmode, *File, *return)
                              // "rw" retained as a request alias — the
                              // native mode parser (mode.rs::parse_open_mode)
                              // treats it as ReadWrite + require-exist,
                              // semantically equivalent to POSIX "r+".
                              // Spec/impl drift on "rw" vs "r+" tracked
                              // as a follow-up; leaving "rw" acceptable
                              // preserves existing caller code.
                              "rw" \/ "w" \/ "w+" \/ "wx" \/ "w+x" \/ "a" \/ "a+" \/ "r+" => {
                                // m-16-6 fix (2026-08-06, M-6 rev-2):
                                // write-capable cap check.  Pre-fix
                                // compared provisioned=="rw" which
                                // never matches for File entries
                                // (CONFIG_FILE_MODES has no "rw"), so
                                // every non-"r" file open was silently
                                // FSERR_UNSUPPORTED.  Post-fix mirrors
                                // File.rho's write-capable-mode gate:
                                // only "r" provisioned rejects; every
                                // other file mode (r+, w, w+, a, a+, rw)
                                // grants write.
                                match provisioned {
                                  "r" => return!(
                                    [false, "FSERR_UNSUPPORTED",
                                     "requested mode exceeds provisioned attenuation"])
                                  _ => openFileImpl!(
                                    canonRoot, "", rel, m, cmode, *File, *return)
                                }
                              }
                              _ => return!(
                                [false, "FSERR_BAD_ARG", "unknown file mode"])
                            }
                          }
                          (_, _, _, "dir", _) => return!(
                            [false, "FSERR_BAD_ARG",
                             "logical name is a directory; use openDir"])
                          _ => return!(
                            [false, "FSERR_IO",
                             "malformed bundle entry"])
                        }
                      }
                    }
                  }
                }
                _ => return!(
                  [false, "FSERR_BAD_ARG", "mode must be a String"])
              }
            }
          }
        }
        _ => return!(
          [false, "FSERR_BAD_ARG", "name must be a String"])
      }
    } |

    // -- openDir(name, options) ------------------------------------
    //
    // Slice 27: fresh-mint per call — same rationale as openFile.
    method openDir(@name, @options) {
      match name {
        n /\ String => {
          new modeCh in {
            match options {
              {} => modeCh!("r")
              {"mode": modeVal ..._} => modeCh!(modeVal)
              {_ : _ ..._} => modeCh!("r")
              _ => modeCh!(Nil)
            } |
            for (@requestedMode <- modeCh) {
              match requestedMode {
                Nil => return!(
                  [false, "FSERR_BAD_ARG", "options must be a Map"])
                m /\ String => {
                  for (@bMap <<- @[*private, *fsBundleP]) {
                    match bMap.contains(n) {
                      false => return!(
                        [false, "FSERR_UNSUPPORTED",
                         "logical name not in static bundle"])
                      true => {
                        match bMap.get(n) {
                          (canonRoot, rel, provisioned, "dir", cmode) => {
                            match m {
                              "r" => openDirImpl!(
                                canonRoot, "", rel, "r", cmode, *File, *return)
                              "rw" => {
                                match provisioned {
                                  "rw" => openDirImpl!(
                                    canonRoot, "", rel, m, cmode, *File, *return)
                                  _ => return!(
                                    [false, "FSERR_UNSUPPORTED",
                                     "requested mode exceeds provisioned attenuation"])
                                }
                              }
                              _ => return!(
                                [false, "FSERR_BAD_ARG",
                                 "dir mode must be \"r\" or \"rw\""])
                            }
                          }
                          (_, _, _, "file", _) => return!(
                            [false, "FSERR_BAD_ARG",
                             "logical name is a file; use openFile"])
                          _ => return!(
                            [false, "FSERR_IO",
                             "malformed bundle entry"])
                        }
                      }
                    }
                  }
                }
                _ => return!(
                  [false, "FSERR_BAD_ARG", "mode must be a String"])
              }
            }
          }
        }
        _ => return!(
          [false, "FSERR_BAD_ARG", "name must be a String"])
      }
    } |

    // -- Default arm ----------------------------------------------
    default(...@args) {
      match args {
        [*ret, ..._] => ret!([false, "FSERR_UNSUPPORTED",
          "unknown method or not implemented in this slice"])
      }
    }
  }
}
